---
// The index idiom, device 1 of 8: the page header — the section title with a
// rule under it, and whatever standing statements the section owns beneath
// that. Rendered by /writing/, /he/writing/ and /projects/ (which takes the
// header and the empty state from this family, and its card grid from
// projects.css) — and by /contact/, which is the minimal archetype and not an
// index at all.
//
// That last caller is a device being reused, not an archetype boundary being
// crossed by accident. contact.astro wrote its header by hand, and the rule
// it wrote was byte-identical to this one: the same 32px of padding under the
// title, the same 1px --border rule, the same five type declarations and
// colour on the h1, value for value. Two devices that merely resembled each
// other would differ somewhere; these did not differ anywhere, which is
// evidence both were transcribed from one prototype device rather than
// authored twice. The header therefore travels alone — nothing else in this
// family goes to /contact/, and the archetypes stay distinct. If the minimal
// archetype's header ever has to differ, it stops calling this and says why.
//
// ── why this family is components ────────────────────────────────────────
//
// entry-list.css opened by explaining that it was ONE stylesheet "so the
// indexes cannot drift — a Hebrew index that looked second-class would break
// specs/pages/translations-index.md §5". That is a correctness property, not a
// filing convention, and utilities written inline at the call sites would have
// spent it: the same token string restated at three call sites is three places
// the indexes can drift apart from each other, with nothing to notice.
//
// So the stylesheet became a component set rather than a set of class
// attributes. A component enforces the shared device at the markup level; the
// stylesheet only enforced it by convention, and inline utilities would not
// have enforced it at all (specs/notes-tailwind-verdict.md §4.1, point 7).
// Eight files for fourteen rules looks like a lot until you count call sites:
// there are eleven of them across the three indexes, and each one now names a
// device instead of restating one.
//
// ── logical properties, throughout the family ─────────────────────────────
//
// `pbe`/`pbs` and `border-be` on the block axis, `ps`/`pe` and `border-s` on
// the inline axis, never the physical `pl`/`pr`/`border-l`. Under dir="rtl"
// the inline axis flips and the block axis does not, so every edge lands on
// the reading start without a special case. That is what makes /he/writing/
// the same page as /writing/ rather than a mirrored port of it, and it is the
// one thing in this family that fails silently: the accent edge on the terms
// callout would simply sit on the wrong side, on a page nobody reads in a
// hurry.
//
// The h1's five type utilities are the display face at --size-h1: this is the
// archetype's title, not a heading level that happens to come first.

interface Props {
  /** The section title. `lang`/`dir` come from the layout's locale, never from here. */
  title: string;
}

const { title } = Astro.props;
---

<header class="pbe-8 border-be border-be-border">
  <h1 class="font-display text-size-h1 font-bold leading-display tracking-display text-text-strong">{title}</h1>
  <slot />
</header>
